```java
class ShortenerService {
    Encoder encoder;
    Repository repository;

    String shorten(String longUrl) {
        String code = encoder.generate(longUrl);
        repository.save(new UrlMapping(code, longUrl, Instant.now(), null));
        return code;
    }

    String resolve(String code) {
        UrlMapping mapping = repository.findByCode(code)
            .orElseThrow(NotFoundException::new);
        if (mapping.isExpired())
            throw new ExpiredLinkException();
        return mapping.longUrl;
    }
}
```

Neither `shorten` nor `resolve` cares which `Encoder` or `Repository` implementation is wired in — the entire encoding-scheme decision on the next slides only changes what's behind the `Encoder` interface, never this class.

```java
class HashEncoder implements Encoder {
    static final int MAX_ATTEMPTS = 5;

    String generate(String longUrl) {
        String salt = "";
        for (int attempt = 0; attempt < MAX_ATTEMPTS; attempt++) {
            String code = truncate(sha256(longUrl + salt), 7);
            if (!repository.existsByCode(code)) {
                return code;
            }
            salt = String.valueOf(attempt);  // perturb the hash input and retry
        }
        throw new EncodingExhaustedException(longUrl);
    }
}
```

The `MAX_ATTEMPTS` bound matters as much as the retry itself — an unbounded retry loop against a persistently colliding namespace (or a bug) could spin forever. Five attempts with a growing salt is enough that a real collision run is astronomically unlikely; hitting the bound signals a deeper problem worth surfacing, not silently looping.
